> **Note:** A more detailed dive on the topic can be found in my old
> site's blog
> [`1k2s.netlify.app`](https://1k2s.netlify.app/blog/understanding-big-o-notation).
> This page goes over the same topic but in a more succinct matter.

Big $O$ Notation is a mathematical notation that describes the behavior of a
value (most commonly denoted as $n$ as it reaches infinity.
Although well defined as such in math, applied in programming it comes in 2 forms **Time
Complexity** and **Space Complexity**.


## Dictionary

1. **Time Complexity**: Used to determine how much time it takes for the CPU to run the code.
2. **Space Complexity**: Memory resources needed to complete the algorithm.
3. **Compile Time**: This is **not** measured with Big O, instead we care about
   runtime, and even though some algorithms can be optimized from
   $O(n)$ to $O(1)$
   (like <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauss_sum">Gauss Sum</a>) for example, this
   blog doesn't go over these kinds of optimizations.
4. **Runtime**: When the program is actually executed. This is what LeetCode
   and other platforms alike measure.

---

## Analysis

Below are the most common algorithm complexities listed, in general it's
important to know that when assessing an algorithm's complexity, we omit
**all** constants and instead focus on how the algorithm works as the input
increases in size.

![Big O Complexities Chart](./big-o/big-o-complexities-chart.png)

### $O(1)$ - Constant Complexity

Seen in:
* Pushing an item to the **back** of a Vector.
* Looking up an item in a Hash Map/Set.
* Getting the first element of a Linked List.

### $O(\log n)$ - Logarithmic Complexity

The concept is as follows: if you divide the amount of steps required by $k$
(most of the time $k=2$) on every iteration, then your algorithm's time
complexity is $O(\log n)$.

Primary use is
[**Binary Search**](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/binary-search/). It is
the gold standard for searching sorted data sets.

### $O(n)$ - Linear Complexity

Described as doing _at least_ one full iteration on your data.

> **Note:** A common trick question is removing the first item of a Vector is
> $O(n)$, unlike removing the last one which is $O(1)$.
> This is due to how Vectors are structured in memory and how every subsequent
> item needs to be moved forward whenever you remove at the first place.

### $O(n \log n)$ - Linearithmic Complexity

Usually where efficient sorting algorithms fall: **Quicksort**, **Heapsort**,
and **Merge sort**, all in regards to time complexity.

### $O(n^2)$ - Quadratic Complexity

Commonly seen in nested loops or matrix traversals (`arr[i][j]`). Bubble Sort also falls here.

> **Note**: Often $O(n^2)$ solutions are referred to as
> [Brute Force](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/brute-force-approach-and-its-pros-and-cons/).
> A common approach to optimizing such problems is called
> [Dynamic Programming](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_programming).

### Complexity Table

In for easy reference you could refer to it:

| Complexity               | Example       | Intuition        |
| ------------------------ | ------------- | ---------------- |
| $O(1)$                   | array access  | constant         |
| $O(\log n)$              | binary search | halves each step |
| $O(n)$                   | linear scan   | proportional     |
| $O(n^2)$                 | nested loops  | pair comparisons |

### Code Implementation Examples

```py
import math

# O(1) - Constant Time
# No matter how large the input 'arr' is, this takes one step.
def constant_example(arr):
    return arr[0] if arr else None

# O(log n) - Logarithmic Time
# Binary Search: Cutting the search area in half each iteration.
def logarithmic_example(arr, target):
    low, high = 0, len(arr) - 1
    while low <= high:
        mid = (low + high) // 2
        if arr[mid] == target:
            return mid
        elif arr[mid] < target:
            low = mid + 1
        else:
            high = mid - 1
    return -1

# O(n) - Linear Time
# A single loop that visits every element once.
def linear_example(arr):
    for item in arr:
        print(item)

# O(n log n) - Linearithmic Time
# Typical for efficient sorts like Merge Sort.
def linearithmic_example(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    mid = len(arr) // 2
    left = linearithmic_example(arr[:mid])
    right = linearithmic_example(arr[mid:])
    return sorted(left + right) # Simplified merge step

# O(n^2) - Quadratic Time
# Nested loops where we compare every element to every other element.
def quadratic_example(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(n):
            print(f"Comparing {arr[i]} and {arr[j]}")

# O(sqrt(n)) - Sublinear Time
# Trial division for primality testing.
def sqrt_n_example(n):
    if n < 2: return False
    for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):
        if n % i == 0:
            return False
    return True
```

